  "worked_solution": "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $2$ wholes. Each whole is split into *sixths*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/ec09e83087ba7ada6af5cf1d2d19e4260a4581f2818659ebedc902f8edf13250.png)\n\n \n\n- The sixths are grouped, and each group contains $\\frac{3}{6}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/c9f6041bf5bb537054adda90744bd54d43f4441b916dd86ca388cb8c2186c324.png)\n\n \n\n- There are $4$ groups in total.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/fa86c95bb607d923b1c1cf23483414960ac541ab5273feda0c89fa6cc4ea6bf2.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $2$ wholes into groups of $\\frac{3}{6},$ we get $4$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\,2\\,}÷\\frac{3}{6}=\\boxed{\\,4\\,}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing numbers are $2$ and $4.$",
